The verb must agree with the main subject, not with a noun appearing in a qualifying/prepositional phrase.

This is basically subject–verb agreement with phrases such as “of + noun.”

1. The box of Nestle's chocolates is missing. ✅

Break it down:

The box → main subject → singular
of Nestle's chocolates → qualifying phrase
chocolates → plural, but not the main subject
is → singular verb

So:

The box of Nestle's chocolates is missing. ✅

2. The boxes of Nestle's chocolates are missing. ✅

Now the main subject is:

The boxes → plural

Therefore:

The boxes of Nestle's chocolates are missing. ✅

Even though chocolates is also plural, the verb agrees with boxes, not chocolates.

3. The decoration of the rooms is beautiful. ✅

Main subject:

decoration → singular

of the rooms is only a qualifying phrase.

Therefore:

The decoration of the rooms is beautiful. ✅

Not:

❌ The decoration of the rooms are beautiful.


From my hotel I could see that a flock of birds were flying over the lake.

The error is:

❌ were flying

The main subject is:

a flock → singular

of birds is a qualifying phrase.

Therefore, according to the rule:

✅ A flock of birds was flying over the lake


The prices of tomatoes has soared... ❌

Main subject:

The prices → plural

Therefore:

✅ The prices of tomatoes have soared...


                                                                        One-third + of



One-third of the pillar has rotted away. ✅

pillar → singular

one-third of the pillar has...

One-third of the pillars have rotted away. ✅

pillars → plural

one-third of the pillars have...

also when two-third is used it should be used as two-thirds

example:
Two-thirds of the pillar has rotted away.

Here the verb depends on the noun after of, because one-third represents a portion of that noun.

So don't turn the rule into:

“Always ignore the noun after of.” ❌

Instead:

Identify the grammatical main subject and determine what the quantity expression refers to.
